#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]
pub enum BalanceError {
    Overflow,
    Insufficient,
}

#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]
pub struct Balance {
    cents: u32,
}

impl Balance {
    pub fn new(cents: u32) -> Balance {
        Balance { cents }
    }

    pub fn value(&self) -> u32 {
        self.cents
    }

    pub fn deposit(&mut self, n: u32) -> Result<(), BalanceError> {
        self.cents = self.cents.checked_add(n).ok_or(BalanceError::Overflow)?;
        Ok(())
    }

    pub fn withdraw(&mut self, n: u32) -> Result<(), BalanceError> {
        self.cents = self
            .cents
            .checked_sub(n)
            .ok_or(BalanceError::Insufficient)?;
        Ok(())
    }

    pub fn transfer(&mut self, n: u32, to: &mut Balance) -> Result<(), BalanceError> {
        let from = self
            .cents
            .checked_sub(n)
            .ok_or(BalanceError::Insufficient)?;
        let into = to.cents.checked_add(n).ok_or(BalanceError::Overflow)?;
        self.cents = from;
        to.cents = into;
        Ok(())
    }
}

problems.description_md:
Sometimes one bad item should not spoil the rest. `recover_tokens(text)` splits `text` at whitespace (`split_whitespace`) and parses each token as a `u32` on its own. It returns a tuple of three things:

1. the values that parsed, in input order;
2. how many tokens failed for any reason other than being too large;
3. how many tokens failed because the number is too large for a `u32`.

A failed parse gives a `ParseIntError`, and its `kind()` method says why, as an `IntErrorKind` (L9.2 Screen 5; Book ch. 9.2, "Matching on Different Errors", does the same with `io::ErrorKind`). Match on the parse `Result` inside the loop; in the `Err` arm, match on `e.kind()`: `IntErrorKind::PosOverflow` counts as too large, and every other kind counts as invalid. `IntErrorKind` may gain variants in later Rust versions, so that inner `match` needs a `_` arm.

Do not stop at a bad token: each error is handled where it happens and the loop goes on, so `?`, which would return at the first error, is the wrong tool here. A token such as `-5` is invalid, not too large: a `u32` has no sign, so `parse` reports `InvalidDigit`.

