Row 48. Reset the generator to seed `0x09030000` once and generate 300 cases. Each case starts from `Library::new()` and adds 4 + pick(5) books with `add_book(T[pick(4)], "Ann", 0)`. It then makes three `add_member(P[pick(4)], "M", premium)` calls, each drawing the id first and then premium = (pick(2) == 1). Then it performs 20 operations; each draws form = pick(12), and n is the number of books at that moment. Forms 0–10 build a command line, call `parse_command(line)` and, when that is `Ok(cmd)`, call `execute(cmd)`:

- form 0: `"add " + AT[pick(4)] + "|Ann|" + Y[pick(5)]` (title drawn first);
- form 1: BADADD[pick(4)];
- forms 2–4: `"checkout {b} {m} {d}"`, where b is 1 + pick(n) when pick(8) is not 0 and otherwise [0, n + 1][pick(2)] (both unknown), then m = MQ[pick(5)] and d = D[pick(5)];
- forms 5 and 6: `"return {b} {day}"`, where pick(4) is drawn first: when it is 0 and some book is borrowed, b is the smallest borrowed id, otherwise b = pick(n + 2); then day = R[pick(8)];
- form 7: FIND[pick(5)]; form 8: REP[pick(3)]; form 9: BAD[pick(8)]; form 10: NL[pick(3)].

Form 11 calls `add_member(P[pick(4)], "M", pick(2) == 1)` directly. The lists are T = `["Rust", "rust book", "東京", "Dune"]`, P = `[1, 2, 1, 4294967295]`, AT = `["Rust", " The  Hobbit ", "", "東京"]`, Y = `["1937", "+7", "65535", "65536", "x"]`, BADADD = `["add Rust|Ann", "add a|b|1|2", "add", "Add Rust|Ann|1"]`, MQ = `[1, 1, 1, 4294967295, 3]` (3 is never a member), D = `["0", "10", "4294967267", "4294967268", "+5"]`, R = `[0, 14, 15, 21, 22, 57, 58, 4294967295]`, FIND = `["find rust", "find  RUST  book ", "find 東京", "find zzz", "find"]`, REP = `["report", " report\t", "report now"]`, BAD = `["", "   ", "checkout 1 2", "return 1", "FIND rust", "checkout 1 1 1 1", "return 1 x", "remove 1"]` and NL = `["find a\nb", "report\r\nreport", "checkout 1 1 5\n"]`.

After the member setup and after every operation, compare the `add_member`, `parse_command` and `execute` results (`Ok` values and `Err` variants with their data) and the snapshot — `count_by_status()`, `book(id)` for every id from 0 to n + 1 and `member(id)` for the ids 1, 2, 4294967295 and 3 — with the independent oracle. The oracle parses with its own tokenizer and the Part 3 rules, and keeps plain lists of books (normalized title, trimmed author, year, status) and members (premium, count), applying the Part 2 order of checks, limits 3 and 6, due day +14 or +28, and fee min(2000, 25·min(late, 7) + 50·max(late − 7, 0)) with late = max(day − due, 0). Realized coverage: `add_member` 699 `Ok` and 686 `DuplicateMember`; `parse_command` 316 `Add`, 1,376 `Checkout`, 1,012 `Return`, 414 `Find`, 336 `Report` and 2,061 `BadCommand`; `checkout` 690 successes, 166 `NoSuchBook`, 372 `NoSuchMember`, 143 `NotAvailable` and 5 `LimitReached`; `return_book` 113 on time, 19 in the 25-cent tier, 61 in the 50-cent tier, 31 capped, 214 `NoSuchBook` and 574 `NotBorrowed`; `Find` 194 lists and 220 `none`. The parser refuses every day above 4294967267, so the `BadCommand` that `checkout` itself returns is reached only by a directly built command (rows 17, 18 and 43). The reference solution and the oracle agree on all 6,300 results and snapshots.

Row 47. Reset the generator to seed `0x06020000` once. Generate cases i=0..299. Each case consumes exactly three draws: member_id=next(), due=next(), raw_day=next(). Let mode=i%10 and premium=(i//10)%2==1. Let limit be 6 for premium, otherwise 3. Book metadata is (id=1,title="x",author="y",year=1); member name is "A". Modes 0–3 start Available and call checkout at today=raw_day%4294967268; initial borrowed is respectively 0, limit, 255, limit-1. Modes 4 and 5 start Lost or Borrowed{member_id,due_day:due}, borrowed=0, then attempt checkout at that same permitted today. Modes 6–9 call return_to_shelf: mode 6 starts Borrowed with matching ID and count 1; mode 7 starts Borrowed with borrower ID member_id XOR 1 and count 1; mode 8 starts Available with count 0; mode 9 starts Borrowed with matching ID and count alternating 0/255 according to (i//10)%2. For return modes override due_day to 100 (the draw is still consumed); choose today from [99,100,101,107,108,143,144,4294967295][(i//10)%8]. The independent oracle uses state and limit comparisons; successful checkout increments once and sets due today+14/28. Rejected operations preserve both complete records. Matching returns produce Some(min(2000,25*min(late,7)+50*max(late-7,0))) with late=max(today-100,0), set Available, and floor the decremented count at zero. Compare the result and complete Book and Member records after every call.

This is the LM6 regression suite, unchanged and carried forward from LM8 through the crate-root re-exports `Book`, `Status` and `Member`, whose methods Chapter 9 does not change. Realized: 60 successful and 120 rejected checkouts, 60 fee-paying and 60 rejected returns; the LM9 reference and the oracle agree on all 300 cases.

